---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="World Eaters Beginner's Guide — Warhammer 40k"
  description="New to World Eaters? Learn who they are, how they play (pure melee chaos), how to paint the red and brass scheme, and exactly what to buy first."
>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 border-b border-[#2a2a35]">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <a href="/factions/" class="text-sm text-[#4a4855] hover:text-[#8a8590] transition-colors mb-6 inline-block">
        ← All Factions
      </a>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-6">
        <div class="text-6xl">🪓</div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2 font-display">Faction Guide</p>
          <h1 class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">World Eaters</h1>
          <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            Pure fury. No restraint. No mercy. The World Eaters are Khorne's chosen — 
            berserker warriors who live to kill, who feel nothing but rage, 
            and who bring chainaxes to everything. The most aggressive army in the game.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick stats bar -->
      <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3">
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Playstyle</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm">All-In Melee</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Painting</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-yellow-400">Medium</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Rules</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-yellow-400">Medium</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Starter Cost</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm">~$170</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Lore -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">Who are the World Eaters?</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg">
        <p>
          Ten thousand years ago, they were the XII Legion — the War Hounds. Elite warriors, ferocious 
          but disciplined, loyal to the Emperor. Their primarch was Angron, a towering figure the Emperor 
          found fighting as a gladiator slave on a distant world. Unlike other primarchs who welcomed 
          the Emperor's arrival, Angron was furious. He had been fighting alongside a band of slave-rebels 
          and the Emperor took him mid-battle — before Angron could die with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          That wound never healed. Angron had also been fitted with neural implants called the 
          Butcher's Nails — devices that suppressed the ability to feel calm or peace, leaving only 
          aggression and pain. He forced them upon his entire legion. Over decades of war, the Nails 
          consumed them all.
        </p>
        <p>
          By the Horus Heresy, the World Eaters were already lost. They pledged themselves to 
          Khorne, the Chaos god of war, blood, and slaughter — the one god who actually rewards 
          the thing they could no longer stop doing. Now they are something worse than warriors. 
          Angron himself was killed and reborn as a Daemon Primarch — a sixty-foot engine of destruction 
          that the Imperium considers one of the most dangerous entities in the galaxy.
        </p>
        <p>
          The World Eaters don't hold territory. They don't have plans. They crash into enemy lines 
          with chainaxes and they kill until there's nothing left. Red armor soaked in blood, 
          brass trim polished on corpses, screaming praise to the Blood God with every swing.
        </p>
        <p>
          One important detail: <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Khorne hates magic.</strong> His followers 
          are too busy killing to think about sorcery. The World Eaters have zero psychic powers — 
          it's a core part of who they are, both in lore and on the table.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Playstyle -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">How they play on the table</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg mb-10">
        <p>
          World Eaters are a <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">pure melee assault army</strong>. 
          Their entire game plan is to cross the table as fast as possible and get into hand-to-hand 
          combat, where they are devastating. They do not win shootouts. They win by making sure 
          there is no shootout.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Khorne Berzerkers</strong> are the beating heart of the army — 
          fearless melee infantry who become more dangerous the longer a fight goes on. 
          <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Jakhals</strong> are cheaper cultist-type warriors who screen 
          your advance and tie up enemy units. The <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Daemon Prince</strong> 
          is a flying melee monster that can threaten almost anything on the table.
        </p>
        <p>
          The challenge of World Eaters is the gap. You need to get across the table through enemy 
          shooting before you can do what you're good at. Positioning your screens, knowing when to 
          charge and what to charge first — that's the skill of the army. Once you're in combat, 
          the game becomes much simpler.
        </p>
        <p>
          Zero psychic abilities. Not a trade-off — it's the whole identity. You are not a subtle army. 
          You are eight feet of screaming daemon-touched Space Marine with a chainaxe, and that's enough.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-green-400">World Eaters are for you if...</h3>
          <ul class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>✓ You want to charge across the table and smash things</li>
            <li>✓ You love the red-and-brass Chaos aesthetic</li>
            <li>✓ You want a focused, aggressive play style</li>
            <li>✓ You like intimidating your opponent on the way in</li>
            <li>✓ You want a smaller, elite-feeling force</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-red-400">Maybe look elsewhere if...</h3>
          <ul class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>✗ You want to sit back and shoot</li>
            <li>✗ You want psychic powers or magic</li>
            <li>✗ You want a lot of tactical variety</li>
            <li>✗ You don't like the idea of being punished for slow play</li>
            <li>✗ You prefer horde armies with lots of cheap models</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Painting -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">How to paint World Eaters</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-8 text-lg">
        Red armor, brass trim, and as much blood as you want. 
        The classic World Eaters scheme is medium difficulty — the red can be fiddly, 
        but the overall look is forgiving and very striking on the table.
      </p>

      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 mb-10">
        <p>
          The main challenge with red is coverage — it's a notoriously thin pigment that can look 
          patchy if you're not careful. The trick is a proper base coat first. Using a red spray 
          primer like Mephiston Red gets you solid, even coverage before you touch a brush. 
          From there, a wash into the recesses and a quick drybrush does most of the work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The brass/gold trim is where World Eaters models really come alive. 
          There's a lot of ornate detail — chains, horns, skulls, armor edging — 
          and getting the brass right transforms a flat-looking model into something imposing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-lg mb-4">The classic scheme — step by step</h3>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">1</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Spray with Mephiston Red</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Citadel's red spray primer. Covers everything in one even pass and gives you a solid red base to build on. Let it dry fully before moving on.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">2</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Wash with Agrax Earthshade</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Apply into all the recesses and panel lines. Adds instant depth and shadow to the armor. You can use Nuln Oil for a cooler, darker look — Agrax gives a warmer result that suits red well.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">3</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Layer Evil Sunz Scarlet on raised surfaces</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">A brighter red applied to the flat panels of the armor (not into recesses). Brings the red back to a vivid tone after the dark wash. Apply carefully to avoid the shadows you just created.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">4</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Paint all trim and detail with Brass Scorpion</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Chains, horns, skull decorations, armor trim — everything metallic gets Brass Scorpion. This is the step where the model starts looking properly World Eaters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">5</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Wash the brass with Agrax Earthshade</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Apply into the recesses of all the brass/gold areas. Tones it down from bright to battle-worn and adds age. Wipe excess off flat surfaces if needed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">6</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Paint chainaxe blades with Leadbelcher, wash Nuln Oil</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Silver metallic on all the blades and chainsaw teeth. A wash of Nuln Oil darkens and dirties them. World Eaters weapons should look used.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">7</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Optional: Blood for the Blood God</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Citadel's technical paint that creates convincing splattered blood. Dab it onto blades, knuckles, and armor. Very optional — but extremely on-brand, and it transforms the model.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-sm mt-4 italic">
          Steps 1–6 give you a fully table-ready World Eaters model. Step 7 is the fun one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- What to buy -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">What to buy first</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-10 text-lg">In order. Don't buy anything else until you've finished step one.</p>

      <!-- Combat Patrol -->
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 mb-4">
          <div>
            <span class="bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] text-xs font-semibold px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-display mr-2">Start Here</span>
            <span class="text-xs text-[#4a4855]">~$170</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-xl mb-2">Combat Patrol: World Eaters</h3>
        <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Contains a Daemon Prince (your flying melee centrepiece), a Master of Executions (a 
          character who buffs your melee output), 10 Khorne Berzerkers (your core infantry — 
          the army's backbone), and 10 Jakhals (cheaper infantry who screen and charge first). 
          22 models total. A legal, playable army straight out of the box.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F5X8GV4B?tag=grimdarkprime-20"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="inline-block bg-[#c9a84c] hover:bg-[#dfc070] text-[#111113] font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded text-sm transition-colors font-display"
        >
          See price on Amazon →
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Expansion -->
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 mb-3">
          <span class="text-xs text-[#4a4855]">~$55 · natural first expansion</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-xl mb-2">Khorne Berzerkers (box of 10)</h3>
        <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          More Berzerkers means more killing power in melee — which is the entire point. 
          This is almost always the right second purchase for World Eaters. The models are 
          excellent and you'll want more of them once you see what they do in a fight.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=khorne+berzerkers+warhammer+40k&tag=grimdarkprime-20"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="inline-block border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] text-[#b8b4c0] hover:text-[#c9a84c] font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded text-sm transition-colors font-display"
        >
          Search Amazon →
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Paints -->
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-xl mb-4">Paints you need</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          {[
            { name: 'Mephiston Red Spray', use: 'Base coat — the red armor foundation', required: true },
            { name: 'Agrax Earthshade', use: 'Shade wash — depth on both red and brass', required: true },
            { name: 'Evil Sunz Scarlet', use: 'Layer — brightens red panels after washing', required: true },
            { name: 'Brass Scorpion', use: 'All the trim, chains, horns, and decoration', required: true },
            { name: 'Leadbelcher', use: 'Blades and weapon metal', required: true },
            { name: 'Nuln Oil', use: 'Wash for metal areas', required: true },
            { name: 'Blood for the Blood God', use: 'Technical paint — gore effects on blades', required: false },
            { name: 'Astrogranite Debris', use: 'Texture paint for basing', required: false },
          ].map(paint => (
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class={`text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0 font-display ${paint.required ? 'bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113]' : 'border border-[#2a2a35] text-[#4a4855]'}`}>
                {paint.required ? 'Essential' : 'Optional'}
              </span>
              <div>
                <span class="text-sm font-semibold">{paint.name}</span>
                <span class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm"> — {paint.use}</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Edition note -->
  <section class="px-6 py-12 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
        <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-[#8a8590]">Edition note:</strong> Warhammer 40,000 releases new editions periodically — 
          specific rules, points costs, and detachment abilities change between them. 
          The lore, models, and painting guides on this page are edition-agnostic. 
          For current rules, check the <a href="https://wahapedia.ru/wh40k10ed/factions/world-eaters/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-[#c9a84c] hover:underline">World Eaters rules on Wahapedia</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="px-6 py-20 text-center">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Blood for the Blood God.</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg mb-8">
        Not sure World Eaters are right for you? Take the quiz — it matches you to a faction based on how you actually want to play.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
        <a
          href="/quiz"
          class="inline-block bg-[#c9a84c] hover:bg-[#dfc070] text-[#111113] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display tracking-wide"
        >
          Take the Faction Quiz →
        </a>
        <a
          href="/factions/"
          class="inline-block border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] text-[#b8b4c0] hover:text-[#c9a84c] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display"
        >
          All Factions
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
